ustrialized  well-off west saying look if ever there  is an occasion on which  a government that has the opportunity to  spend money on vaccines a government  that has the opportunity to spend money  to prioritize its own citizens  with this would be that occasion  yeah i mean it's completely  understandable listen my dad got  vaccinated on friday  i'm in that group that might be affected  by the delays and some of the rollout  that's being predicted  um but i really think it's a false  choice that's being presented there  our governments remember we're the ones  that have paid for these vaccines  we finance their creation uh we paid  upfront a huge amount of money billions  of pounds handed over to the  pharmaceutical industry  to pay for the manufacturer of those  doses and so it's really in our power  if we choose to make efforts to scale up  manufacturing capacity around the world  and unfortunately that's the other part  of the this um  problem that we're seeing right now that  isn't being properly addressed are we  doing everything we could  to make sure we're producing every  possible vaccine that could be  and right now we know that there are  manufacturers from canada to bangladesh  who have said  we could step up we could produce more  and vaccines we could be rolling out  millions of doses in addition to what's  being produced already  if we had permission and we had the  know-how that was was required to  do that and unfortunately our government  when they were negotiating those deals  and handing over billions of  our money uh to the companies didn't  secure that commitment to share the  vaccine  openly with as many manufacturers as  possible so we're seeing bottlenecks and  shortages now that don't need to exist  and the uk government whilst they've  done  some really good things in terms of the  support for the kovacs facility  and really are going as far as they need  to in terms of pushing the  pharmaceutical industry  to share the intellectual property  rights the permission basically to make  these vaccines  and sharing the know-how that would  allow other labs to come on stream  and we know that if we did do that we'd  overcome some of the shortages we're  seeing  right now yeah i i'm really sorry gents  giving giving events actually  events not too far away from darwin  speaking to us in in bristol last night  i'm so sorry but we have to leave this  conversation hopefully we'll be able to  return to it  in the days to come donald uh wilfred  gurupira thank you so much for joining  us  for the time that we did have for you uh  well let's just look at the home  secretary um  well of course she has sharply condemned  those scenes in bristol last night  after the protest against government  plans to increase police powers  descended into violence and left  several officers in hospital now let's  head back to bristol and speak  to the chief constable there andy norsch  of avon and  somerset police uh chief constable great  to have you on the program tonight  this morning um i wish the circumstances  were somewhat  different and i suspect you feel the  same just just take us through what  what you and your officers saw take  place in bristol last night  so most days in bristol over the last  few weeks and months we've seen protests  yesterday was no different but the  gathering was much bigger  so up to 3 000 people gathered at  college green they marched down through  the city  and a group of four or 500 ended up  protesting where i'm standing now  outside the bride world police station  and let's remember this is a  neighborhood place this is a base where  our team to protect the most vulnerable  people in bristol work  and some disgraceful and violent scenes  emerged  12 of our cars were damaged the cars  that helped us protect those vulnerable  people  20 of the brave officers who were trying  to quell that violence and deal with  that disorder of being injured  two of them seriously we've made seven  arrests and we've now launched a very  significant investigation  which is going to see these criminals  brought to justice and very soon later  today  we will be asking for your help to  identify those responsible  and chief constable i wonder if we can  just speak very specifically about the  the two officers who faced serious  injury can can you give us any kind of  an update as to their condition  well i spoke to one last night i believe  that email will have a collapsed  or punctured loan very seriously injured  stamped on i believe  and another officer with broken bones  fortunately all of our people are out of  hospital now i'm very pleased to say  but it was a terrifying scene and i'm  sure on the media  social media you will have seen  neighborhood offices police community  support officers  effectively trapped inside this building  with people on the roofs firing  fireworks at them hurling projectiles at  them  and the other officers outside doing  their very best to protect everyone  present  it really was a disgraceful scene  committed by criminals  i don't believe this was a a protest  this wasn't people  frustrated at the lack of ability to  protest this was people  intent on causing serious disorder  violence and damage  people with a grudge against policing i  would say just to be absolutely clear  from what you've just said there and  we're looking at the pictures where  we're just looking at the pictures from  last night where your officers were very  very clearly  you know in considerable amounts of risk  at times yesterday you feel  that that protest yesterday was what was  hijacked some people elsewhere have  suggested  that the phrase rent a mob the phrase  phrase usual suspect has  has cropped up that's your understanding  as to what took place last night  the people involved in assaulting the  officers burning out vehicles damaging  vehicles smashing windows  hurling projectiles from the  multi-storey car park you can see behind  me  throwing things at our horses and dogs  yes they were people who came for a  fight with the police  we did everything that we could to try  and calm the situation  to try not to get drawn into a trigger  point that would have caused  maybe people who are wondering should i  get involved shouldn't i so we did  everything we could to disperse that  crowd to encourage them to leave  but ultimately unfortunately it turned  to violence at about 5 30 to 6 p.m  do you think that this will have any  effect on the way in which uh even and  somerset police  police events of this type and future so  we're we're  i have incredibly experienced and  skillful police officers at every level  um very capable of dealing with protest  and protest is all about context  some people have said it's the brain  surgery of policing so there  is no simple a to z guide do this  everything will be okay  our objective is always to prevent  violence and disorder  sometimes that frankly isn't possible  and last night was one of those moments  the people that took their opportunity  to be violent to injure the police  officers  to smash up the building behind me they  will be arrested and they will be  brought to justice  of course we look to learn from every  single event we're involved in  but i want to pay tribute to the brave  men and women of having the some second  strawberry  they did their very best last night they  were superb indeed and i i wonder though  do you understand at all the concerns  that some people have  about freedom of expression freedom of  association freedom of protest that  that some people feel that whilst we  deal with covet 19  those priorities have have perhaps been  ignored by the government and  and the police find themselves in the  invidious position of having to  to police people who simply want to be  out there making a point  so it's been an incredibly difficult  year i've been in the police for 34  years i never ever thought that i would  be policing the sort of restrictions  on private lives that we've placed with  great care  and sensitivity throughout this pandemic  indeed and even in somerset our  objective has been to come out of the  pandemic  closer to the public that we're here to  serve and in large extent  we believe we've achieved that we  believe that what happened last night  is not about the people of bristol  that's not what this city is about  that's not what we stand for  so it is incredibly difficult but i'm a  police officer  it's my job to enforce the law and  police the law which is passed by  parliament  chief constable andy marsh great to see  you this morning thank you very much for  joining us  thank you did you know  that reducing pollution outside of  school can help stop  toxic air getting trapped in our  classrooms  [Music]  some fine weather today with some warm  sunny spells temperatures higher than  recent days across the southeast as  we've lost that northerly breeze  but there will be areas of cloud  particularly across the north and west  thick enough  for a little bit of drizzly rain near  exposed western coast and temperatures  will be  a little bit lower across the north and  west through the day  now as we look at air pollution the  levels are low  just about everywhere but in more urban  areas and  busy roads i think it could well be  moderate at times but generally speaking  quite good air quality  in the week to come the air quality  report  sponsored by phillips air purifiers  uh let's get our money's worth from our  political correspondent tom rayner this  morning tom  we've been speaking to the care minister  helen waitley not the most senior member  of government to appear in the program  but she has given us an idea of of where  this battle with the european union is  going next  yes and i think it's going in the  diplomatic direction as opposed to a  ratcheting up of tensions given what  she said this morning the front pages  indicate that we look set to be in what  amounts to a sort of